fn\">x</span></span>)</span></span></div></div>","info":{"doc":"","docLink":"./Init/Data/Bool.html#Bool.instDecidableForallOfDecidablePred","kind":"instance","line":34,"name":"Bool.instDecidableForallOfDecidablePred","sourceLink":"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/11acb17ec6b07a8f9e9173e6845197929540936b/src/Init/Data/Bool.lean#L34-L39"}},{"header":"<div class=\"decl_header\"><span class=\"decl_kind\">def</span>\n<span class=\"decl_name\"><a class=\"break_within\" href=\"./Init/Data/Bool.html#Bool.«term_^^_»\"><span class=\"name\">Bool</span>.<span class=\"name\">«term_^^_»</span></a></span><span class=\"decl_args\"> :</span><div class=\"decl_type\"><a href=\"./Init/Prelude.html#Lean.TrailingParserDescr\">Lean.TrailingParserDescr</a></div></div>","info":{"doc":"Boolean “exclusive or”. `xor x y` can be written `x ^^ y`.\n\n`x ^^ y` is `true` when precisely one of `x` or `y` is `true`. Unlike `and` and `or`, it does not\nhave short-circuiting behavior, because one argument's value never determines the final value. Also\nunlike `and` and `or`, there is no commonly-used corresponding propositional connective.\n\nExamples:\n * `false ^^ false = false`\n * `true ^^ false = true`\n * `false ^^ true = true`\n * `true ^^ true = false`\n\n\nConventions for notations in identifiers:\n\n * The recommended spelling of `^^` in identifiers is `xor`.","docLink":"./Init/Data/Bool.html#Bool.«term_^^_»","kind":"def","line":30,"name":"Bool.«term_^^_»","sourceLink":"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/11acb17ec6b07a8f9e9173e6845197929540936b/src/Init/Data/Bool.lean#L30-L30"}},{"header":"<div class=\"decl_header\"><span class=\"decl_kind\">abbrev</span>\n<span class=\"decl_name\"><a class=\"break_within\" href=\"./Init/Data/Bool.html#Bool.xor\"><span class=\"name\">Bool</span>.<span class=\"name\">xor</span></a></span><span class=\"decl_args\"> :</span><div class=\"decl_type\"><span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"./Init/Prelude.html#Bool\">Bool</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Prelude.html#Bool\">Bool</a> → <a href=\"./Init/Prelude.html#Bool\">Bool</a></span></div></div>","info":{"doc":"Boolean “exclusive or”. `xor x y` can be written `x ^^ y`.\n\n`x ^^ y` is `true` when precisely one of `x` or `y` is `true`. Unlike `and` and `or`, it does not\nhave short-circuiting behavior, because one argument's value never determines the final value. Also\nunlike `and` and `or`, there is no commonly-used corresponding propositional connective.\n\nExamples:\n * `false ^^ false = false`\n * `true ^^ false = true`\n * `false ^^ true = true`\n * `true ^^ true = false`\n\n\nConventions for notations in identifiers:\n\n * The recommended spelling of `^^` in identifiers is `xor`.","docLink":"./Init/Data/Bool.html#Bool.xor","kind":"def","line":15,"name":"Bool.xor","sourceLink":"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/11acb17ec6b07a8f9e9173e6845197929540936b/src/Init/Data/Bool.lean#L15-L28"}}],"imports":["Init.NotationExtra"],"instances":[{"className":"Decidable","name":"Bool.instDecidableForallOfDecidablePred","typeNames":[]},{"className":"Decidable","name":"Bool.instDecidableExistsOfDecidablePred","typeNames":["Exists"]},{"className":"LE","name":"Bool.instLE","typeNames":["Bool"]},{"className":"LT","name":"Bool.instLT","typeNames":["Bool"]},{"className":"Decidable","name":"Bool.instDecidableLe","typeNames":["LE.le"]},{"className":"Decidable","name":"Bool.instDecidableLt","typeNames":["LT.lt"]},{"className":"Max","name":"Bool.instMax","typeNames":["Bool"]},{"className":"Min","name":"Bool.instMin","typeNames":["Bool"]},{"className":"Std.Commutative","name":"Bool.instCommutativeAnd","typeNames":[]},{"className":"Std.Commutative","name":"Bool.instCommutativeOr","typeNames":[]},{"className":"Std.LawfulIdentity","name":"Bool.instLawfulIdentityBeqTrue","typeNames":["Bool.true"]},{"className":"Std.LawfulIdentity","name":"Bool.instLawfulIdentityBneFalse","typeNames":["Bool.false"]},{"className":"Std.Associative","name":"Bool.instAssociativeBne","typeNames":[]}],"name":"Init.Data.Bool"}